<Site title="Ifá — Sacred knowledge, readings and oral literature" active="Learning">
<article class="wrap narrow article-section ifa-guide">
<header><p class="kicker">LIVING KNOWLEDGE · YORÙBÁ HERITAGE</p><h1>Understanding Ifá</h1><p class="lead">A signature is only the beginning. What makes Ifá a living tradition is the knowledge, poetry, interpretation and responsibility carried by its practitioners.</p>
<figure><img src="/images/orunmila-ifa-tray.webp" width="1100" height="1204" alt="Artistic illustration of a carved divination tray, palm nuts and a divination chain" loading="lazy"/><figcaption class="fine">AI-generated illustration, not a photograph of a documented shrine or ceremony.</figcaption></figure></header>
<nav class="pills" aria-label="Ifá guide sections"><a href="#knowledge">Sacred knowledge</a><a href="#instruments">Instruments</a><a href="#reading">Readings</a><a href="#mathematics">Mathematics</a><a href="#verses">Oral literature</a></nav>
<section id="knowledge"><h2>More than a divination sign</h2><p>Ifá is a Yoruba system of divination and oral knowledge associated with Ọ̀rúnmìlà. Babaláwo and, in traditions recognising women in this role, Ìyánífá learn a body of narratives and verses through training. Questions of Orí (personal destiny) and ìwà pẹ̀lẹ́ (gentle or good character) belong to a wider religious and ethical conversation. Community practices are not identical.</p><p>UNESCO recognises the living divination tradition and its extensive oral corpus. This guide introduces cultural knowledge; it is not an initiation, consultation or personal oracle.</p><a href="/figures/orunmila/">Meet Ọ̀rúnmìlà →</a></section>
<section id="instruments"><h2>The instruments and their purposes</h2>
<dl class="glossary"><div><dt lang="yo">Ọ̀pẹ̀lẹ̀</dt><dd>A divination chain with eight elements arranged in two groups of four. The two faces of each element give distinguishable outcomes, producing a signature.</dd></div><div><dt lang="yo">Ikin</dt><dd>Sacred palm nuts used by trained diviners. A commonly documented method associates one remaining nut with a double mark and two with a single mark. Handling and ritual requirements belong to the practitioner tradition.</dd></div><div><dt lang="yo">Ọpọ́n Ifá</dt><dd>A carved tray on which divination marks may be recorded. Some trays feature a carved face associated with Èṣù; designs and explanations differ.</dd></div><div><dt lang="yo">Ìyẹ̀ròsùn</dt><dd>Fine divination powder used as a surface for tracing marks; preparation and material descriptions should be attributed to specific practitioners.</dd></div><div><dt lang="yo">Ìrókẹ́ Ifá</dt><dd>A divination tapper associated with invocation and the tray.</dd></div><div><dt lang="yo">Ìbò</dt><dd>Objects used in further inquiry. Their assignments and selection procedures depend on practice and lineage.</dd></div></dl></section>
<section id="reading"><h2>What happens during a reading?</h2><ol><li>Opening observances honour sacred powers and the practitioner's tradition.</li><li>A signature is obtained through a recognised divination instrument.</li><li>The diviner identifies the Odù and draws on associated ẹsẹ Ifá, rather than assigning a single keyword to the pattern.</li><li>Further inquiry, including ìbò in relevant practices, may clarify favourable or unfavourable circumstances.</li><li>Interpretation may lead to ethical counsel, practical action or a ritual prescription such as ẹbọ. Not every consultation follows an identical sequence.</li></ol>
<h3>Ire and Ibi</h3><p>Ire refers to favourable possibilities, such as longevity, livelihood or offspring. Ibi may refer to adverse circumstances, such as loss, illness or conflict. Terms and classifications vary. A reading is a religious practice, not a medically validated diagnosis or a guarantee of future events.</p><p>Ìbò can help a trained practitioner refine a question, but no universal cowrie-versus-bone assignment is asserted here. Ẹbọ is a religious offering or sacrifice; its meaning and application are understood within the relevant tradition.</p><a href="/figures/esu/">Explore Èṣù and mediation →</a></section>
<section id="mathematics"><h2>Two marks, 256 possibilities</h2><p>Each of four positions can take one of two states. There are 2⁴ = 16 possibilities for one column and 16 × 16 = 256 ordered combinations of two columns. Sixteen have matching columns and 240 have different columns. In modern mathematics this is equivalent in cardinality to eight bits, but a classroom encoding is not the ritual itself.</p>
<div class="ifa-example" aria-label="Illustrative ordered single and double mark columns"><div><strong>Left column</strong><pre>Ⅱ\nⅡ\nⅡ\nⅡ</pre><code>0000</code></div><div><strong>Right column</strong><pre>Ⅰ\nⅠ\nⅠ\nⅠ</pre><code>1111</code></div></div>
<p>In this illustrative convention, a single stroke is 1 and a double stroke is 0. Reading the right column first gives 11110000. Reversing the columns gives 00001111, a distinct ordered configuration. This example is not a personal reading. The existing matrix uses classroom positions, not authenticated sign-to-name codes.</p><p>Modern binary arithmetic and Ifá share a mathematical structure. That does not establish a historical transmission from Ifá to Leibniz or modern computers.</p><p><a class="button" href="/learning/ifa/odu/">Browse the 256 Odù signatures</a></p><a class="button secondary" href="/learning/ifa-matrix/">Explore the 256 mathematical pairings</a></section>
<section id="verses"><h2>Ẹsẹ Ifá: a library carried in memory</h2><p>The 256 Odù organise a much larger body of oral literature. A signature can call to mind many verses, narratives and precedents, and trained interpretation matters. A single fixed theme for each Odù would misrepresent that richness. We will preserve attributed versions, original Yoruba wording, translations and the context of a particular telling when permission and provenance are established.</p><p>No invented sacred verse is presented as inherited knowledge. The proposed Ogbè–Ọ̀yẹ̀kú longevity account supplied for editorial review needs a named source before publication as an authentic verse.</p><p>Ask an elder or practitioner which accounts they learned, from whom, and whether the account may be shared publicly. Some knowledge may be restricted.</p></section>
